  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests model-capacity and compute scaling for reinforcement-learning agents and reports performance in that setting, but it does not test language-model cross-entropy power laws or the other claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports RL task returns as model width, depth, or update-to-data ratio changes. It does not measure held-out language-model loss, fit or extrapolate power laws for that loss, or identify a fixed-compute optimum split. References to scaling laws in other work are not results from this study.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites Kaplan et al. and argues that supervised scaling laws often fail in RL. Its own results are RL task returns (normalized return, success scores) as critic width, critic depth and UTD ratio vary. It does not measure held-out loss or fit or extrapolate a power law. It does not find a compute-optimal split, and no version's power-law form is tested. This is a related but different construct (monotone return gains in RL), so it does not test V1–V7; the reader's 'tests/T3' contradicts its own all-'not_tested' version list.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct: the authors conclude that SimbaV2's hyperspherical normalization and distributional critic with reward scaling stabilize optimization, so SAC scales with larger critics and higher UTD ratios without resets. Simba plateaus. They draw no conclusions about language-model loss power laws.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates an RL architecture using task returns, critic width and depth sweeps, and update-to-data ratios; it does not use the language-model loss-scaling measurement or fitting procedure.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Performance varies by benchmark and scaling axis: width scaling is stronger for SimbaV2 than Simba on HBench-Hard, and DMC-Hard depth performance begins saturating around L = 4.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "With SimbaV2, RL return rises with critic width (0.3M–17.8M params), depth (saturating near L=4 on DMC-Hard) and UTD ratio (DMC-Hard IQM 0.713→0.851→0.849 at UTD 1/4/8). Simba plateaus or degrades. No power-law fit.",
        "proposed_claim": "In off-policy continuous-control RL, task return improves with critic size and update-to-data ratio only when the architecture stabilizes norms; gains saturate within the tested range.",
